   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Provincial Department of Labour and Vocational training (PDOLVT) Migrant resources centre (MRC) in Kampong Thom, in collaboration with the  Provincial Department of Women&apos;s Affairs, conducted workshops for service providers and local authorities, including the district governor and district judicial polices, to share knowledge and experiences as well as strengthen coordination. 122 (33 W) participants were informed about challenges women migrant workers WMWs experience, policies to protect migrant workers (MW), and services that local authorities are responsible for.
Cambodian Women Crisis Center (CWCC) formed 6 Community Based Protection Networks (CBPNs) in Phnom Penh and Kandal. The CBPNs strengthened migrant community support networks, raised awareness about safe migration, and empowered members to take an active role in addressing exploitation, discrimination, violence and harassment experienced by MWs.  121 WMWs and their families became CBPNs members. CWCC provided members with knowledge of safe migration, violence against women (VAW), trafficking; and directories of relevant service providers. The CBPNs were also mentored to conduct awareness-raising sessions and provide socio-emotional support to other members.  As a result, CBPN members reached out to 337 WMWs and families (276 W) with information on safe migration, VAW, and TIP. 
5 MRCs expanded strategies to provide specific support to WMWs and mainstream issues of gender, VAW and TIP into their regular services. 1,174 MWs and their families benefitted from case management, safe migration and psycho-social counselling, and community outreach activities. The National Employment Agency (NEA), PDOLVT, and  CWCC, integrated COVID-19 preventive messages into outreach activities. 1425 (1107 women) MWs and their families received relevant information and services. 
&quot;Saver Asia&quot;- a website and mobile application for MWs to access remittance services &#x2013; was delivered.
 With support from the European Union, the ILO has provided resources through i) 4 implementation agreement with the NEA and PDOLVTs to operate 4 MRCs in Kampong Thom and Siem Reap, and ii) one implementation agreement with CWCC to establish and operate a mobile MRC covering Kandal and Phnom Pen Kampong Thom, and organized WMWs and their families into CBPNs. Through this, the ILO has been able to support the provision of specific services for women migrant workers, as well as mainstream services that look into issues of gender, VAW, violence and harassment into the regular programming of local service providers. The ILO has also provided facial masks and hand sanitizers to MRC staff to support the continued operation and staff safety during the COVID-19 pandemic. 

To ensure sustainability and user uptake of SaverAsia, TRIANGLE in ASEAN, together with NEA and Developing Markets Associates Global, organized with 45 managers and officials (M:30; W:15) from 13 Job Centres. The participants discussed remittances and different ways to send and receive money, learn to use the SaverAsia app and develop an outreach plan to promote SaverAsia. The SaverAsia Cambodia Facebook page reached 512,790 viewer, and an instructional guide was adapted into Khmer.</narrative>
   </description>
