  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">With support from the Cambodian Ministry of Labour and Vocational Training (MOLVT), the Association of Cambodian Recruitment Agency (ACRA) and Manpower Association of Cambodia (MAC) furthered a new framework on fair and ethical recruitment by jointly adopting of the Code of Conduct (COC) for Cambodian Private Recruitment Agencies in January 2020 .
The adoption of COC is a significant new commitment of the recruitment agencies, with Government support, to improving and delivering fair and ethical recruitment for migrant workers (MWs). The observable change is that there is now a guide for the recruitment practices of these private recruitment agency associations that was negotiated through extensive consultation with social partners and discussion on aligning with ILS standards, including those on non-discrimination and recruitment costs specifically.

To enhance women&apos;s access to training and apprenticeship opportunities, the  Ministry of Tourism (MOT) developed standard operating procedure for apprenticeship in Cambodia (SOPAC) that consist of 16 apprenticeship manuals aligned with the common ASEAN Tourism Curriculum (CATC) qualification levels, and recognition of prior learning RPL assessment tools.  Following this, MOT collaborated with 5 skills centres (run by Civil Society Organisations)  to train potential and returnee women migrant workers (WMWs).  Finally, 84 potential and returnee WMWs in hotel services were assessed for recognition of prior learning (RPL) in Siem Reap through work by the Ministry of Tourism. Workers who have gained on the job experience in Cambodia or abroad and who have not gone through formal education can attain certification of their skills through RPL assessments. Building on work completed in the 2018-19 biennium, and with support from the Government of Canada and Australia, the ILO supported launch of the Code of Conduct (CoC) in January 2020, with further development of the pilot programme and capacity building of key recruitment agency representatives ongoing in 2020 and 2021, including participation at ITCILO recruitment training. The ILO is providing ongoing technical support to develop the processes and pilot programme for assessing compliance with the CoC, alongside the key recruitment agency associations. 

With support from the European Union, the ILO provided technical inputs to support the development of the standard operating procedure for apprenticeship in Cambodia  (SOPAC) and apprenticeship manuals, as well as the conduct of trainings for (52) trainers in Phnom Penh and Siem Reap. The ILO also collaborated with Ministry of Tourism (MOT) to develop the Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) assessments and to hold preparatory and analysis workshops with the assessors.</narrative>
   </description>
